\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A stable negative quadratic term}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The [coercive potential class](fdip-00GU) includes some negative
quadratic terms. Let #{s\ge1}, every #{\lambda_v>0}, and let #{K}
be any real symmetric matrix in
#{U(q)=q^TKq/2+\sum_v\lambda_vq_v^4/24}. Write
#{K_-} for the positive negative part of #{K}, and put
##{\lambda_* =\min_v\lambda_v,\qquad
 c=\frac{\lambda_*}{24s},\qquad d=\frac{\|K_-\|}{2}.}
Since #{\sum_vq_v^4\ge|q|^4/s},
##{U(q)\ge c|q|^4-d|q|^2
 \ge a|q|^2-\frac{(a+d)^2}{4c}\qquad(a>0).}
The second inequality completes the square in #{|q|^2}:
#{cr^2-(a+d)r\ge-(a+d)^2/(4c)}. Thus any chosen #{a>0}
gives a finite admissible #{b}.}

\p{A fixed Wick-adjusted quartic may therefore satisfy precisely
the hypotheses of \ref{fdip-00GW}. The displayed constants depend
on its actual finite potential; this calculation supplies no
cutoff-uniform bound.}
